23:08:33 [f07f] The social questions are the PRIORITY: exactly 35 of the 50 questions must come from this social-communication guidance, covering a diverse mix of its targets:
23:08:33 [f07f] --- SOCIAL-COMMUNICATION GUIDANCE (knowledge base) ---
23:08:33 [f07f] Social level (ages 8): Peer-interaction and social-communication targets appropriate for an older child who is beginning to manage school, recess, group activities, friendship, emotional understanding, and self-advocacy demands.
23:08:33 [f07f] Set questions in these contexts: school, recess, home, group activity, friendship, game play, therapy session.
23:08:33 [f07f] Avoid: forcing eye contact; punishing autistic communication style; violent or unsafe peer scenarios; complex social inference without story support; assuming social goals from diagnosis alone
23:08:33 [f07f] Cover a diverse mix of these social-communication targets (do not repeat the same one):
23:08:33 [f07f] - reciprocal conversation: Starting, maintaining, and ending a conversation with a peer. Skills: greeting, asking a relevant question, answering on topic, trading information.
23:08:33 [f07f] - peer entry and group joining: Joining an ongoing peer activity or group interaction. Skills: watching what the group is doing, waiting for an appropriate moment, asking to join, joining without interrupting.
23:08:33 [f07f] - friendship development: Understanding how to make and maintain friendships. Skills: finding common interests, choosing appropriate peers, inviting someone to play, being a good host.
23:08:33 [f07f] - emotion understanding: Recognizing and discussing emotions in self and others. Skills: identifying basic emotions, linking emotions to causes, noticing facial, body, and context cues, expressing one's own feeling.
23:08:33 [f07f] - perspective taking theory of mind: Understanding that another person may think, know, feel, or want something different. Skills: identifying another person's belief, understanding different preferences, predicting how someone may react, explaining why someone acted a certain way.
23:08:33 [f07f] - cooperative play and group participation: Participating in games, group tasks, and shared activities. Skills: following game rules, taking turns, sharing materials, being flexible.
23:08:33 [f07f] - conflict resolution: Handling disagreement with peers in a safe and socially functional way. Skills: staying calm, using words to explain the problem, listening to the other person, suggesting a solution.
23:08:33 [f07f] - teasing bullying and rejection response: Responding to teasing, exclusion, or bullying safely. Skills: distinguishing playful joking from hurtful teasing, using a short response, walking away, seeking adult support.
23:08:33 [f07f] - social problem solving: Understanding a social problem and selecting an appropriate response. Skills: identifying the problem, thinking of possible solutions, predicting consequences, choosing a safe solution.
23:08:33 [f07f] - self advocacy and communication of needs: Expressing needs, boundaries, preferences, and requests for help. Skills: asking for a break, saying no appropriately, asking for clarification, explaining sensory needs.
23:08:33 [f07f] - Use these targets to support functional participation, self-advocacy, emotional understanding, peer interaction, and mutual communication.
23:08:33 [f07f] - Do not force eye contact, masking, or neurotypical-style social behavior.
23:08:33 [f07f] - Use concrete school, recess, home, therapy, and peer-play scenarios.
