23:57:42 [6d10] Wording: Questions can be longer and can ask for explanation, comparison, or reasoning, but should remain concrete and contextually grounded. Phrase every question as ONE simple spoken clause a child would hear in conversation; split complex ideas into the question frames below instead of packing them into one sentence.
23:57:42 [6d10] Question length: 8-14 words when expressive language level supports it.
23:57:42 [6d10] Avoid: fully abstract reasoning without concrete context; adult-level nutrition or biology mechanisms; technical food production; highly ambiguous category exceptions; complex social inference without story support; academic connector phrases such as 'in terms of', 'with respect to', 'in what way'; two asks packed into one question (e.g. 'How are X and Y alike in terms of what they are made from?' — use 'What are X and Y both made from?' instead)
23:57:42 [6d10] Use these simple question frames (one clause each): How are X and Y alike? | Why does X belong to this category? | Can X belong to two categories? | What is X made from? | Why did the character do that? | What happened after X? | What should the character do next?
23:57:42 [6d10] Target these concepts (vary across the question set):
23:57:42 [6d10] - cross classification: Ask whether one item can be classified in multiple valid ways. e.g. Is an apple both fruit and food?
23:57:42 [6d10] - superordinate subordinate reasoning: Ask whether an item is a kind of another category, or whether one category is broader than another. e.g. Is a poodle a kind of dog?
23:57:42 [6d10] - origin and processing reasoning: Ask about natural source, animal source, ingredient source, or simple processing pathway. e.g. Do apples grow on trees?
